 "coverage": "80 ICs and 250 nets over 958 pins, from DICE's netlist of the game. DICE is an emulator: its netlist is the circuit as it simulates it, at the board positions the drawings use, and is not a drawing of the board. Where it stands an RC delay or a speed hack in a wire, the wire is restored; what it models that is not a chip on the board (CLOCK_14_318_MHZ, COIN_INPUT, DIPSWITCH, LATCH, PADDLE1_VERTICAL_INPUT, PADDLE2_VERTICAL_INPUT, PADDLE3_VERTICAL_INPUT, PADDLE4_VERTICAL_INPUT, …) is left off the board view, and the resistors and capacitors are not named at all. Supply pins are joined to +5V and GND from each part's pinout. The ICs sit at their board positions, on the grid spacing of Atari's boards.",
